VALUES ('2a9c2924-47e0-4116-95f1-585235a66d9b', 'secplus-practice-1', 'CompTIA Security+ (SY0-701) Practice Paper 1', 'comptia-sec-plus', ARRAY['CIA Triad', 'Encryption', 'Social Engineering', 'Zero Trust', 'SIEM', 'Incident Response', 'MFA', 'Risk Management']::TEXT[], 20, 'standard', true, 1, 30);

-- ── Domain: General Security Concepts (12% — 2 questions) ──

VALUES ('2e1b0ceb-ed68-41e3-80a8-270bdc469815', '2a9c2924-47e0-4116-95f1-585235a66d9b', 1, 'A company''s customer database is altered by an attacker who is not authorized to modify it, but the data remains accessible and readable to legitimate users the whole time. Which component of the CIA triad was violated?');
VALUES ('2e1b0ceb-ed68-41e3-80a8-270bdc469815', '01d06ca1-5f1e-4ebd-ba1b-785869013514', 'The CIA triad''s three components map to specific, distinct concerns: Confidentiality means only authorized parties can READ the data (not violated here — the data was still only viewable by legitimate users); Integrity means the data has not been improperly created, altered, or destroyed — this is exactly what was violated, since an unauthorized party modified the data; Availability means authorized users can access the data when needed (not violated here — the data remained accessible throughout). Correctly mapping a specific scenario to the specific triad component it violates — not just recognizing "this is bad, so it must violate all three" — is a foundational, frequently-tested Security+ skill.');

VALUES ('743b6d76-7827-4a60-943e-6a476fa40d3c', '2a9c2924-47e0-4116-95f1-585235a66d9b', 2, 'A company needs to encrypt a large volume of data at rest efficiently, and separately needs a way for two parties who have never met to securely exchange a secret key over an insecure channel. Which statement correctly matches encryption type to each need?');
VALUES ('743b6d76-7827-4a60-943e-6a476fa40d3c', '8ba1f24a-ea14-4367-8280-65eeddd3736b', 'Symmetric encryption (same key encrypts and decrypts) is computationally efficient and well-suited to bulk data-at-rest encryption, but requires both parties to already possess the shared secret key — which is exactly the problem asymmetric encryption (a public key anyone can encrypt with, and a mathematically linked private key only the recipient holds, used to decrypt) solves: two parties who have never met can exchange information (or negotiate a symmetric session key, as TLS actually does in practice) without a pre-shared secret. Asymmetric encryption is significantly slower for large volumes of data, which is why real systems (like TLS) typically use asymmetric encryption only for the initial key exchange, then switch to fast symmetric encryption for the actual bulk data. Hashing is a one-way function (not reversible/decryptable at all) and is unrelated to key exchange — a common and important distinction from encryption.');

-- ── Domain: Threats, Vulnerabilities & Mitigations (22% — 4 questions) ──

VALUES ('7288ed4a-bed3-45ba-9826-acb22281bef3', '2a9c2924-47e0-4116-95f1-585235a66d9b', 3, 'An attacker sends a highly personalized email to a company''s CFO specifically, referencing real internal project names and impersonating the CEO, requesting an urgent wire transfer. Which social engineering term most precisely describes this specific attack?');
VALUES ('7288ed4a-bed3-45ba-9826-acb22281bef3', '0fc2740d-4ac2-45d0-b222-33de4db945bf', 'Security+ tests a specific hierarchy of social engineering terms, and precision matters: phishing is the broad category (fraudulent, often mass-sent email); spear-phishing narrows this to a specific, researched, personalized target; whaling narrows further to specifically targeting high-profile individuals (executives, "big fish") — exactly matching a CFO being targeted with a personalized, researched pretext referencing real internal details. Vishing is voice/phone-based social engineering (not email), and tailgating is a PHYSICAL security attack (following an authorized person through a secured door) — neither matches an email-based attack, which is why precisely matching the attack vector and target specificity to the correct term (not just "it''s phishing, good enough") is the actual tested skill.');

VALUES ('43739e74-23d3-4ea7-803d-aa4a525c6a6a', '2a9c2924-47e0-4116-95f1-585235a66d9b', 4, 'A web application constructs SQL queries by directly concatenating user-submitted form input into the query string. An attacker submits a crafted input that alters the query''s logic to bypass authentication. What is the vulnerability class, and what is the standard mitigation?');
VALUES ('43739e74-23d3-4ea7-803d-aa4a525c6a6a', 'e9066d86-b316-4eaa-a7f7-277cc30b2e70', 'This is the textbook mechanism of SQL injection: unsanitized user input is concatenated directly into a SQL query string, letting an attacker craft input that changes the query''s actual logic (a classic example: entering `'' OR ''1''=''1` into a login field to make the WHERE clause always evaluate true, bypassing authentication). The standard, correct mitigation is parameterized queries/prepared statements, where user input is bound as a literal DATA value in a fixed query structure and can never be interpreted as executable SQL syntax, regardless of what the input contains — input sanitization/escaping is a weaker, error-prone secondary defense, not the primary recommended fix. XSS involves injecting malicious script that executes in OTHER users'' browsers (a different vulnerability class, unrelated to database queries). A buffer overflow involves writing past allocated memory bounds (a memory-safety issue, unrelated to SQL). This scenario has nothing to do with request volume/rate, ruling out denial-of-service.');

VALUES ('f23ee357-7145-4ce6-9623-2ac4c2f9f9cd', '2a9c2924-47e0-4116-95f1-585235a66d9b', 5, 'A security team wants to identify known, catalogued security weaknesses in their systems (e.g., missing patches, outdated software versions) using automated tooling, WITHOUT actively attempting to exploit anything or simulate a real attacker''s full attack chain. What is this activity called, and how does it differ from penetration testing?');
VALUES ('f23ee357-7145-4ce6-9623-2ac4c2f9f9cd', '2c7955f9-ded6-45bb-9376-b1d7ef32c4d4', 'Vulnerability scanning is specifically the automated, non-exploitative identification of known weaknesses (missing patches, outdated versions, misconfigurations) against a database of known vulnerabilities (like the CVE database) — it tells you WHAT might be wrong. Penetration testing goes further: testers (often human-led, sometimes combined with automated tools) actively attempt to exploit identified (and sometimes not-yet-catalogued) weaknesses to demonstrate real, chained impact — it tells you what an attacker could actually DO with those weaknesses. This distinction (identify vs. actively exploit) is one of the most frequently tested Security+ concept pairs. Digital forensics is post-incident evidence collection/analysis, unrelated to proactively finding weaknesses. Threat hunting is proactive, hypothesis-driven searching for signs of an ALREADY-present, undetected attacker — a different activity from either vulnerability scanning or pen testing.');

VALUES ('01a3d765-82b8-4c90-922f-6a7b4d2d20bb', '2a9c2924-47e0-4116-95f1-585235a66d9b', 6, 'A user reports their files have all been renamed with an unfamiliar extension and are inaccessible, alongside an on-screen message demanding cryptocurrency payment for a decryption key. What type of malware is this, and what is the immediate recommended first response?');
VALUES ('01a3d765-82b8-4c90-922f-6a7b4d2d20bb', 'b0b7b6a3-0238-4089-9cf1-3f06ec991af0', 'File encryption plus an extortion demand for a decryption key is the defining behavior of ransomware. The immediate, correct first response is containment — isolating the infected system from the network (unplugging it, disabling its network interface) to prevent lateral spread to other systems — followed by the organization''s formal incident response process; paying the ransom is explicitly NOT the recommended first response (it funds further attacks, doesn''t guarantee actual decryption, and may violate sanctions/legal requirements depending on jurisdiction and the threat actor). A worm is malware that self-replicates across a network (a possible DELIVERY mechanism for ransomware, but not the correct name for this encryption-and-extortion behavior itself). A rootkit hides its presence/maintains privileged access stealthily — it does not typically encrypt files for ransom. A Trojan disguises itself as legitimate software to gain initial access — again, a possible delivery mechanism, not the correct term for the encryption-extortion behavior described, and disk defragmentation has no security function whatsoever.');

-- ── Domain: Security Architecture (18% — 4 questions) ──

VALUES ('0a44d3c6-faed-4087-a089-1637e29be79d', '2a9c2924-47e0-4116-95f1-585235a66d9b', 7, 'A company adopts a security model based on the principle "never trust, always verify" — no user or device is automatically trusted based solely on being inside the corporate network perimeter; every access request is authenticated and authorized individually, regardless of origin. What is this security model called?');
VALUES ('0a44d3c6-faed-4087-a089-1637e29be79d', '540ad533-eccf-4725-a7f4-0e7018ea75a9', 'Zero Trust is defined by exactly this principle — "never trust, always verify" — rejecting the older assumption that anything inside the corporate network perimeter is inherently safe. Every request, whether from an internal or external source, is individually authenticated and authorized (often continuously, not just at initial login) based on identity, device posture, and context, rather than network location alone. This is the direct opposite of legacy perimeter-based security, which trusted anything "inside the firewall" by default — a model increasingly considered insufficient given remote work, cloud services, and insider threats. SSO is an authentication convenience feature (one login for multiple systems) — it can be a COMPONENT within a Zero Trust implementation, but is not itself the same concept as Zero Trust, and "full trust architecture" isn''t a genuine industry term.');

VALUES ('3c958027-a2fe-4e31-ae50-7176909a857c', '2a9c2924-47e0-4116-95f1-585235a66d9b', 8, 'A company wants to host a public-facing web server while keeping their internal corporate network isolated from direct internet exposure, such that even if the web server is compromised, the attacker does not gain direct access to internal systems. What network architecture concept addresses this?');
VALUES ('3c958027-a2fe-4e31-ae50-7176909a857c', '12d58abc-5ef9-42ab-9b54-2387e2a841b7', 'A DMZ is a network segment specifically designed to host public-facing services (web servers, mail servers) in a zone that is reachable from the internet but is separated, via firewalls, from the internal corporate network — this containment means that if the public-facing server is compromised, the attacker lands in the DMZ, not directly in the internal network, and still has to breach an additional firewall boundary to reach internal systems. This is a concrete application of network segmentation, a core defense-in-depth principle. Placing a public-facing server directly on the flat internal network (option B) is exactly the anti-pattern a DMZ exists to prevent — a compromised server would then have direct network-level access to internal systems. Firewalls are foundational to a DMZ''s entire purpose, not irrelevant to it.');

VALUES ('37072e88-cb89-4932-8351-32a205d24b08', '2a9c2924-47e0-4116-95f1-585235a66d9b', 9, 'A security architecture relies on multiple independent, overlapping security controls (firewall, endpoint antivirus, network segmentation, user training, MFA) rather than a single strong control, such that if one layer fails or is bypassed, other layers still provide protection. What principle is this?');
VALUES ('37072e88-cb89-4932-8351-32a205d24b08', 'c3596e4f-8d61-4b90-82d4-f259d074b84b', 'Defense in depth is precisely this layered-controls principle: no single security control is assumed to be perfect or unbreakable, so multiple independent layers (network, endpoint, user awareness, identity/access) are combined such that a failure or bypass of any one layer is still caught by another — the opposite of relying on one strong perimeter. Security through obscurity is a different (and generally discouraged as a PRIMARY strategy) concept — relying on secrecy of implementation details rather than genuine layered controls. This scenario describes the direct opposite of a single point of failure, which is exactly the failure mode defense in depth is designed to avoid. Least privilege is an access-control principle (granting only the minimum permissions needed for a role), a distinct concept from layering multiple different TYPES of controls.');

-- ── Domain: Security Operations (28% — 6 questions) ──

VALUES ('002669c6-2b06-427d-b91b-00980451645c', '2a9c2924-47e0-4116-95f1-585235a66d9b', 10, 'A security team wants a centralized system that aggregates log data from firewalls, servers, and endpoints across the organization, correlates events across those sources, and generates real-time alerts on suspicious patterns. What type of tool is this?');
VALUES ('002669c6-2b06-427d-b91b-00980451645c', '531a8c34-9346-4d1c-beee-76949eca499b', 'A SIEM is purpose-built for exactly this: ingesting and centralizing log/event data from many disparate sources (firewalls, servers, endpoints, applications), correlating events across those sources to detect patterns a single log source alone wouldn''t reveal (e.g., a failed login on one system followed by unusual data access on another), and generating real-time alerts — this correlation-across-sources capability is the SIEM''s defining, differentiating function. None of the other three options (a single antivirus agent, a Network ACL, a password manager) have any log-aggregation or cross-source-correlation capability at all — each serves a completely different, narrower security function on its own.');

VALUES ('ea2613c4-89f8-49b5-9bbc-a70ec16d97a1', '2a9c2924-47e0-4116-95f1-585235a66d9b', 11, 'A security team has confirmed an active breach is underway. Per standard incident response methodology, what is the correct order of the response phases, starting immediately after detection?');
VALUES ('ea2613c4-89f8-49b5-9bbc-a70ec16d97a1', '76408652-ee89-49c6-94e6-7dfafcd43c13', 'The standard incident response lifecycle, immediately after identification/detection, is: Contain (stop the incident from spreading further — e.g., isolate the affected system from the network — this must happen FIRST, before anything else, to limit ongoing damage), then Eradicate (fully remove the threat''s root cause — malware, unauthorized access, the vulnerability that was exploited), then Recover (restore affected systems to normal, verified-clean operation), and finally Lessons Learned (a post-incident review to improve detection, response, and prevention going forward). Doing these out of order defeats the purpose of each phase — for example, attempting Eradication or Recovery before Containment risks the threat continuing to spread or re-infecting a just-recovered system, and skipping straight to Lessons Learned before actually responding makes no operational sense.');

VALUES ('2e264f8a-67dd-4d29-a4be-37357e96c3ea', '2a9c2924-47e0-4116-95f1-585235a66d9b', 12, 'A login system requires a user to enter their password AND a one-time code generated by an authenticator app on their phone. What authentication concept does this represent, and which two factor categories are actually being combined?');
VALUES ('2e264f8a-67dd-4d29-a4be-37357e96c3ea', '781212b5-6625-42e7-bd8b-d921e9e44222', 'Security+ tests the three classic authentication factor categories: "something you know" (a password, a PIN), "something you have" (a physical or virtual possession — a phone running an authenticator app, a hardware token), and "something you are" (biometrics — fingerprint, facial recognition). MFA specifically requires combining factors from genuinely DIFFERENT categories — a password (know) plus an app-generated time-based one-time code tied to a specific device (have) is a textbook two-factor example. Using two passwords, or a password plus a security question, would NOT count as true MFA, since both are still "something you know" — this "different categories, not just multiple steps" distinction is a frequently tested nuance. Biometric authentication specifically means "something you are," which isn''t what''s described here. Federated identity refers to using one identity provider to authenticate across multiple separate systems/applications (like "Sign in with Google") — an entirely different concept from combining authentication factors.');

VALUES ('d9e73dbe-10e8-4fc0-90d4-e361b3f4f035', '2a9c2924-47e0-4116-95f1-585235a66d9b', 13, 'A new employee in the accounting department is granted access only to the specific financial systems and files their role actually requires, and nothing more — not broad access to every department''s systems "just in case." What access control principle is this?');
VALUES ('d9e73dbe-10e8-4fc0-90d4-e361b3f4f035', '688c73f9-9d76-433e-9526-e2669d17cb11', 'Least privilege is the specific principle of granting a user (or system/process) only the minimum access rights needed to perform their actual required function — nothing broader "just in case," which directly limits the potential damage from a compromised or misused account. Mandatory access control (MAC) and discretionary access control (DAC) are both broader ACCESS CONTROL MODELS (how access decisions are made and by whom — MAC is centrally enforced by policy/labels, DAC lets resource owners grant access at their discretion) — either model CAN be used to implement least privilege, but neither IS the same concept as least privilege itself, and least privilege isn''t exclusive to one specific model over the other. "Maximum privilege" is not a real, recommended security principle — it directly describes the anti-pattern least privilege exists to prevent.');

VALUES ('4e188e4c-fb2b-4149-8fe4-7eb5bc4fa560', '2a9c2924-47e0-4116-95f1-585235a66d9b', 14, 'A digital forensics investigator collects a hard drive as evidence from a compromised server. To ensure the evidence remains legally admissible, what documentation practice must be maintained throughout every step of the investigation?');
VALUES ('4e188e4c-fb2b-4149-8fe4-7eb5bc4fa560', 'f2a03f3c-a4f5-46d0-b727-f14e6981b8b7', 'Chain of custody is the specific forensic documentation practice required for evidence to remain legally admissible: every single person who handled the evidence, when, where, and exactly what was done (collected, transported, analyzed, stored) must be recorded in an unbroken, verifiable chain — any gap or undocumented handoff creates a legitimate basis to challenge the evidence''s integrity in court, potentially making it inadmissible regardless of whether tampering actually occurred. NDAs and SLAs are legal/business agreements about confidentiality and service commitments respectively — neither establishes evidentiary integrity or admissibility. "Trust the investigator" is explicitly NOT sufficient in a legal context; documented, verifiable process is what the legal system actually requires, independent of any individual''s personal trustworthiness.');

VALUES ('11a47279-42f8-4029-a57e-691818cea9dd', '2a9c2924-47e0-4116-95f1-585235a66d9b', 15, 'Traditional signature-based antivirus fails to detect a new malware variant because it doesn''t match any known signature in its database. A newer class of endpoint security tool instead monitors process behavior in real time (e.g., a Word document spawning PowerShell, which then attempts to disable security tools) to catch this kind of threat. What is this newer tool category called?');
VALUES ('11a47279-42f8-4029-a57e-691818cea9dd', '98a8934e-f3b3-4c60-97f8-8309c1e7daff', 'EDR tools are specifically designed to address signature-based antivirus''s core limitation — they don''t just check files against a known-bad list, they continuously monitor process behavior, system calls, and activity patterns on the endpoint, flagging suspicious BEHAVIOR (like an Office document spawning a script interpreter, which then attempts to disable security tooling — a classic attack chain) even when no specific file matches a known malware signature, since the malware itself may be brand new or deliberately obfuscated. This behavioral, real-time-response capability is EDR''s defining advantage over traditional signature-based antivirus, which this question directly contrasts. A firewall filters network traffic by IP/port/protocol — it has no visibility into what''s happening INSIDE a single endpoint''s running processes. A VPN provides encrypted network tunneling for remote connectivity — also unrelated to endpoint process monitoring.');

-- ── Domain: Security Program Management & Oversight (20% — 4 questions) ──

VALUES ('217ea96e-4702-4ce2-977f-2b7b608ea0db', '2a9c2924-47e0-4116-95f1-585235a66d9b', 16, 'A risk assessment report distinguishes between a "threat," a "vulnerability," and the resulting "risk." A company has an unpatched web server (a known software flaw) and is aware that ransomware groups actively target unpatched servers of exactly this type. Which statement correctly matches each term to this scenario?');
VALUES ('217ea96e-4702-4ce2-977f-2b7b608ea0db', 'a215ce82-56b9-4550-a57e-f3cd7fcddd7d', 'This three-term distinction is foundational to Security+ risk management content and is frequently tested precisely because people conflate the terms in casual speech: a vulnerability is a WEAKNESS in a system (the unpatched flaw itself — it exists whether or not anyone is trying to exploit it); a threat is a potential SOURCE of harm capable of exploiting a vulnerability (the ransomware groups actively targeting this exact flaw); and risk is the combination of likelihood and impact that results from a specific threat actually exploiting a specific vulnerability against a specific asset — risk is what a risk assessment actually tries to quantify and prioritize (e.g., via a risk matrix), and it''s genuinely possible to have a vulnerability with no realistic threat (low risk) or a threat with no matching vulnerability (also low risk) — risk requires BOTH to combine.');

VALUES ('0d480b91-5312-467c-a705-a2e969617f0d', '2a9c2924-47e0-4116-95f1-585235a66d9b', 17, 'A healthcare organization must comply with a specific regulatory framework governing the protection of patient health information. Which compliance framework specifically applies to this?');
VALUES ('0d480b91-5312-467c-a705-a2e969617f0d', '3133049c-09c7-4a7a-8521-796472117b1c', 'HIPAA is the specific US federal law governing the privacy and security of Protected Health Information (PHI) — matching a healthcare organization''s compliance obligation exactly. PCI-DSS is a DIFFERENT framework specifically governing payment card data (credit/debit card information) — a common Security+ distractor precisely because both are frequently-tested, industry-specific frameworks that shouldn''t be confused with each other. GDPR is the EU''s broad data protection regulation covering personal data generally (not healthcare-specific, and not the "only" framework globally — many countries and industries have their own frameworks, including HIPAA in the US specifically for healthcare). Compliance frameworks like HIPAA carry real legal force with genuine penalties for violations — they are not voluntary for organizations that fall under their scope.');

VALUES ('23253ff6-4e9f-43d3-9c17-3981c02ec8b1', '2a9c2924-47e0-4116-95f1-585235a66d9b', 18, 'A company runs mandatory annual training simulating phishing emails sent to employees, teaching them to recognize suspicious links and reporting procedures. What security control category does this belong to, and why is it emphasized despite having strong technical controls (email filtering, EDR) already in place?');
VALUES ('23253ff6-4e9f-43d3-9c17-3981c02ec8b1', '11a10362-c83b-41a5-9c35-16768b5c4c95', 'Security+ classifies controls broadly into technical (firewalls, EDR, encryption — implemented via technology), administrative/managerial (policies, training, procedures — implemented via process and people), and physical (locks, badges, cameras — implemented via physical means) categories; recurring phishing-simulation training is a security awareness program, an administrative/managerial control. It''s emphasized specifically because technical controls like email filtering and EDR, however strong, cannot catch every possible attack — a sufficiently well-crafted, targeted phishing email can slip past automated filters, and the last line of defense at that point is a human recognizing it and not clicking/reporting it instead — this is why "humans are the weakest link" is a common security phrase, and why layering administrative controls (training) alongside technical ones is standard defense-in-depth practice, not redundant. It has nothing to do with the training''s physical location.');

VALUES ('27766f16-9e68-4884-af7f-fe70cd890d28', '2a9c2924-47e0-4116-95f1-585235a66d9b', 19, 'A company''s data center suffers a fire, physically destroying servers. The company needs a plan for BOTH restoring critical business functions quickly (potentially from an alternate site) AND separately, a longer-term plan for rebuilding full IT infrastructure. Which two distinct planning concepts map to these two needs?');
VALUES ('27766f16-9e68-4884-af7f-fe70cd890d28', 'e297be92-dd7d-4c39-8131-6f0116d1f15e', 'BCP and DR are related but distinct, frequently-tested concepts: Business Continuity Planning has the broader scope of keeping essential BUSINESS functions operating through a disruption — this might mean staff temporarily working from an alternate site, falling back to manual/paper processes, or using a backup vendor, even before full IT systems are restored. Disaster Recovery has the narrower, IT-specific scope of actually restoring technical systems and infrastructure (servers, data, applications) to normal operating condition — DR is typically considered a SUBSET of the broader BCP effort, specifically the technology-recovery piece. This scenario''s two needs (quickly restoring critical business functions vs. separately rebuilding full IT infrastructure) map directly onto BCP''s and DR''s genuinely different scopes, respectively — treating them as identical, or reversing which one covers IT vs. general business operations, are both common test traps.');

VALUES ('9c1630f4-2922-48c8-818b-f4ae91d29bcc', '2a9c2924-47e0-4116-95f1-585235a66d9b', 20, 'An organization decides that the cost of implementing a specific security control exceeds the potential financial impact of the risk it would address, so they formally document the decision to accept the risk and proceed without the control. What risk response strategy does this describe?');
VALUES ('9c1630f4-2922-48c8-818b-f4ae91d29bcc', '9b04f4b7-7ea4-4370-ad72-73b342093522', 'Security+ tests four standard risk response strategies: Avoidance (eliminating the risk entirely, often by not engaging in the risky activity at all — not what''s described, since the underlying activity/system continues); Transference (shifting the risk to a third party, e.g., via cyber insurance or outsourcing — not described, since no third party is involved here); Mitigation (actively reducing the risk''s likelihood or impact via a control — the opposite of what''s happening, since the company explicitly decided NOT to implement the control); and Acceptance (a deliberate, DOCUMENTED decision to take no further action because the cost of addressing the risk exceeds the risk''s potential impact) — this last one exactly matches the scenario: a conscious, formal, ongoing choice to own the residual risk rather than spend more mitigating it further.');
